WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.988 --> 00:00:07.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:12.450
Hi, I'm Athanasia Mowinckel,
but everyone just calls me Me.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:16.920
And today, I'd like to talk to
you about how I made a package,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.290
and in that process,
made some friends.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.790
So to know a little
bit about me,

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.180
I have a background
in psychology

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.270
with a specialization
in neuroimaging.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.610
And in this field,
there's quite a lot of us

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:34.220
who do a lot of coding but we
have no formal training in it,

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.840
so sharing our code
is kind of scary.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.660
At least for me, it was
completely out of the question

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.390
putting my scripts
publicly online.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:47.650
I would just share them with
my closest collaborators.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.800
I mean, why is it so scary
to put your scripts online?

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:57.260
Well, you have this idea that
other people know what they're

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.840
doing so when they
see your code,

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.000
they're just going to think
it's like this sloppy mess that

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.320
is completely useless.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.260
And they're just going to throw
all these negatives at you.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.960
And in the end, the
end message will

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:17.300
come that you have no business
doing what you're doing,

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:18.350
and it's terrifying.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:24.890
But that changed a
little bit over time.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.990
And that started when
my colleague Didac

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.800
approached me and asked me if
we could plot brains ggplot2.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.540
So I'm obsessed with ggplot2.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.940
I plot everything
in it if I can.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:36.860
Or I force myself
to plot everything

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.990
in it no matter what.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.030
So he had this idea.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.570
And he just turned
around and made it happen

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:50.280
and suddenly we had
brains in ggplot2.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.960
And it was like this--

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.710
it was such a cool process to
be able to create our stats

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.890
and immediately
project them onto areas

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:03.550
of the brain in a way that
we were put into publication

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.380
had not been able to
do that R before.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:10.180
And I thought it's not just
R labs that needs this.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.690
We have international
colleagues that we know also

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:13.940
could use something like this.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:17.860
So maybe this is a
time to figure out

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:22.150
how to share your code.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:24.830
So Didac
gave me his script.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:28.180
And I turned the
script into a function.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.120
And I took that one function,
turned into many functions

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:32.950
in a package.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:37.640
And I shipped the
package off to GitHub.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.380
And I was terrified.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:49.400
Excited, but terrified of
what people would think.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.030
But it turns out I
didn't have to be.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.580
Because people started
tweeting about it like,

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:56.320
Mara started tweeting about

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.550
it, and other neuroscientists
that I respect a lot

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:03.160
started tweeting
about the package

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:08.680
being really good and easy to
use, giving them pretty plots.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:13.600
And suddenly, all this
unequivocally positive feedback

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.390
that I'd never gotten
in academia before.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:21.460
It was so exciting
and thrilling.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:25.660
And it was so much fun having
made something that was useful.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.150
I never had anything
useful before.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.220
And it was just so cool.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.700
The next thing that
happened was even cooler,

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:42.580
because people started doing
pull requests to our packages.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:45.070
People we didn't
know from before

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:49.060
and they helped us improve
the efficiency of our functions,

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:52.960
making sure they passed
R command checks,

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.100
adding unit tests.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:58.110
I had never heard of unit test,
sorry, before that PR came.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:59.860
And suddenly, I was
obsessed about writing

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:03.580
unit tests for everything.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:06.920
And this user, John
Shelly from Johns Hopkins,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.520
he helps us prepare our
package to be included

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.950
in the neuro conductor,
which is a bio

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:14.830
conductor for neuroimaging
that we didn't even know

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:17.560
existed before this.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:22.570
And suddenly, we were connected
to this network of R developers

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:25.340
for neuroimaging that
we didn't know before.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:29.680
And we have this
reciprocal community.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:32.680
And it's so extremely cool.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.550
And now, I'm not so scared
about sharing my code

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:37.720
and putting it online.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.670
I do it quite often in my
blog, and in the multitude

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:47.710
of packages I've made
since that first one.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:51.250
It's just nice to connect with
people who do and appreciate

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:52.570
the same things you do.

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:58.380
So make a package and
make some friends.

